size management is handled by Redis itself or by controlling the lifespan of entries.
1. The "Hard Limit" (Redis Server Level)
    You set a global memory limit on the Redis server. When this limit is reached, Redis will automatically delete old data to make room for new data based on an eviction policy.

    Configuring via redis.conf:
    --------------------------- 
        maxmemory 256mb
        maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru  # Deletes Least Recently Used keys first


    Configuring at runtime via CLI:
    -------------------------------- 
        CONFIG SET maxmemory 256mb
        CONFIG SET maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru

2. Time-Based Size Control (TTL)
    Since Spring Boot cannot limit the number of keys in Redis, the standard approach is to set a Time-To-Live (TTL). This ensures that entries are eventually removed, preventing the cache from growing indefinitely. 

    You can set a global default or per-cache TTLs in your RedisCacheConfiguration bean:
        @Bean
        public RedisCacheManager cacheManager(RedisConnectionFactory connectionFactory) {
            // Default TTL for all caches
            RedisCacheConfiguration defaultConfig = RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig()
                .entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(10));

            // Custom TTL for specific "users" cache
            Map<String, RedisCacheConfiguration> customConfigs = new HashMap<>();
            customConfigs.put("users", RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig()
                .entryTtl(Duration.ofHours(1)));

            return RedisCacheManager.builder(connectionFactory)
                .cacheDefaults(defaultConfig)
                .withInitialCacheConfigurations(customConfigs)
                .build();
        }

3. Eviction Policies (The "Which to Delete" Rule)
When your maxmemory is hit, you choose a strategy for what to kick out: 
   - allkeys-lru: (Recommended) Removes the least recently used keys regardless of whether they have a TTL.
   - volatile-lru: Only removes keys that have an expiration (TTL) set.
   - allkeys-lfu: Removes the least frequently used keys.
   - noeviction: Returns an error when memory is full (risky for production). 


In Spring Boot, before (or as an alternative to) using caching annotations like @Cacheable, developers used RedisTemplate to manage cache logic manually. This programmatic approach requires you to write the "check-then-act" logic yourself. 

Manual Caching Logic Flow
Without annotations, your service method follows these manual steps:
    - Check: Manually query Redis to see if the data exists.
    - Hit: If found, return the data immediately (often requires manual casting/serialization).
    - Miss: If not found, query the primary database, then manually save that result into Redis for next time. 

Example Code with RedisTemplate
    Here is how you would implement a service using RedisTemplate in a modern Spring Boot 3.x project (2026):

    @Service
    public class ProductService {

        @Autowired
        private RedisTemplate<String, Object> redisTemplate;

        @Autowired
        private ProductRepository productRepository;

        public Product getProduct(Long id) {
            String key = "PRODUCT_" + id;

            // 1. Manually check the cache
            Product cachedProduct = (Product) redisTemplate.opsForValue().get(key);

            if (cachedProduct != null) {
                System.out.println("Cache Hit!");
                return cachedProduct;
            }

            // 2. Cache Miss - fetch from DB
            System.out.println("Cache Miss! Fetching from DB...");
            Product product = productRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();

            // 3. Manually store in Redis with a TTL (e.g., 10 minutes)
            redisTemplate.opsForValue().set(key, product, Duration.ofMinutes(10));

            return product;
        }
    }

Key Differences from Annotations
    - Granular Control: You can decide exactly when to cache data and set different expiration times (TTL) for every single entry, which is harder to do with static annotations.
    - No Proxy Issues: Annotations rely on Spring AOP proxies and sometimes fail if you call a @Cacheable method from within the same class. Manual code always works regardless of where it is called.
    - Complexity: You must handle potential null checks, serialization errors, and cache key naming manually.
    - Data Structures: While annotations mostly use simple Key-Value pairs, RedisTemplate gives you direct access to advanced Redis types like Hashes, Lists, Sets, and Sorted Sets (using opsForHash(), opsForList(), etc.). 


